\title{Soil Carbon Five Years after Woodland Creation on Former
Sheep Pasture: Early Evidence from One Estate}
\author{E.~Macaulay and J.~Ferreira}
\date{April 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
Woodland creation on former pasture is expected to rebuild soil
carbon, but early changes are rarely measured directly. Five years
after planting on one estate in the Scottish Borders, we compared
soil carbon stocks under the new woodland with adjacent grazed
fields. Topsoil carbon was about nine percent higher under the
woodland, with no detectable difference at depth. We discuss the
limits of single-estate, short-window evidence for guiding planting
policy.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}
Governments are funding woodland creation on agricultural land at an
unprecedented scale, partly on the promise of soil carbon gains.
Yet most evidence comes from chronosequences and modelling rather
than direct measurement in the first years after planting, when
soils are most disturbed. This note reports measured stocks five
years after planting on one estate, against the grazed fields that
surround it.

\section{Site and Sampling}
The estate lies on gently sloping ground in the Scottish Borders and
was under sheep pasture for at least four decades before planting.
Sampling covered the new woodland and two adjacent fields that
remain grazed. Cores were taken to 30~cm in autumn, split at
10~cm, and bulk density was estimated from a regional soil survey
because no plot-level measurements were available.

