\section{Conclusion}
Five years after planting, the topsoil under new woodland already
holds measurably more carbon than the surrounding pasture, while
deeper layers show no change. Whether the early gain persists, and
how far it generalises beyond this estate, remains to be tested with
longer monitoring across more sites.

\end{document}

\section{Results}
Topsoil carbon stocks (0--10~cm) were higher under the new woodland
than under the adjacent pasture (31.2 versus 28.4~Mg~C~ha$^{-1}$, a
difference of about nine percent). At 10--30~cm the two land uses
were nearly identical (44.6 versus 45.1~Mg~C~ha$^{-1}$).

